      <text:p text:style-name="P55">Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure6" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">7</text:sequence>: Cell F Subscribes to Cell E in Domain D<text:span text:style-name="T218">2</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">If there were no tangential interest in domain D<text:span text:style-name="T218">2</text:span> for cell E stimuli, the final way to pull stimuli from domain D<text:span text:style-name="T218">2</text:span> into domain D<text:span text:style-name="T218">1</text:span> would be the use of a bridge cell. <text:s/><text:span text:style-name="T228">A bridge cell maintains a presence in multiple domains and uses subscriptions to pull stimuli across domain boundaries.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Frame19" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:x="1.0665in" svg:y="0.0283in" svg:width="4.202in" draw:z-index="32">
